<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amon of Judah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">7th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amon was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> for about two years in the mid-7th century BCE, the son and successor of Manasseh. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> says he continued his father's religious policies and was murdered by his own servants in the palace. The people of the land killed the conspirators and placed his eight-year-old son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josiah">Josiah</a> on the throne.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amon-of-judah.jpg" alt="Amon of Judah" width="320" height="318" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Published by Guillaume Rouillé (1518?-1589) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amon_rex.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>According to the biblical account, Amon was the fifteenth king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>. He was the son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manasseh-of-judah">Manasseh of Judah</a> and Meshullemeth, a daughter of Haruz of Jotbah, and he married Jedidah, the daughter of Adaiah of Bozkath, on a date that is unknown. He began to reign at the age of 22 after Manasseh's death. Other kings seem to have been made co-regents at about the age of 12, but his reign allows no period of co-regency even though his father was very old. This, with Manasseh's age of 44 at his birth, suggests he may have been a younger son.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Amon reigned for two years. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> records that he continued his father's idolatry and sacrificed to all the images Manasseh had made. Later Jewish tradition judged him harshly: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Talmud</a> says he burnt the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a> and let spider webs cover the altar, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> describes his reign in similar terms to the biblical accounts. Arnold J. Toynbee took a different view and thought Amon and Manasseh nearer to historical truth as pious conservatives defending the faith of their ancestors.</p><p>His reign fell at a time of transition in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-assyrian-empire">Neo-Assyrian Empire</a>, to the east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>, was beginning to disintegrate, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Neo-Babylonian Empire</a> had not yet risen. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psamtik-i">Psamtik I</a> was recovering from the Assyrian occupation. In this power vacuum many smaller states, Judah among them, could govern themselves without interference from larger empires.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Amon was assassinated by his servants or officials, who conspired against him. The murderers were unpopular with the people of the land, who killed them and proclaimed Amon's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josiah">Josiah</a>, then eight years old, as his successor. It is not clear what succession the officials had intended. Some scholars, such as Abraham Malamat, hold that he was killed because people disliked the heavy influence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-assyrian-empire">Neo-Assyrian Empire</a> over him. Out of respect for Josiah, rabbinic tradition did not place Amon on the list of kings excluded from the world to come, and the Gospel of Matthew names him in the genealogy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Amon of Judah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Kingdom of Judah. Died in Jerusalem, in what is now <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.78,35.23&amp;year=-635" aria-label="Jerusalem on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5458 1007 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Amon of Judah"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5908 1277h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5908 1277h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=31.78,35.23&amp;year=-635">What was here in 635 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
